# Cache-Control for kolwen.com (LWK-134a). Parsed by Workers, NOT served as an asset.
#
# The images and the icon here are NOT content-hashed, so the doc's `immutable, max-age=31556952`
# must never be copied onto them: a regenerated brand mark would stay stale in caches for a year.
# These are finite and short enough that a redeploy is visible the same day. Only the font files
# below are content-hashed, and only they may carry `immutable`.
/*.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/*.svg
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400
/*.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# The font files ARE content-hashed: each name carries the first ten hex digits of the sha256 of its
# bytes (scripts/font-subset.mjs writes it, scripts/font-versions.test.mjs holds name and bytes
# together), so changed bytes are a new url and a year in every cache is safe. The patterns name the
# .woff2 files only. fonts.css, the one unhashed file that names them and that every page links,
# and the OFL.txt licence texts keep the platform default (revalidated), because their urls never
# change. One rule per folder with one splat each, and no other Cache-Control rule matches these
# paths: a header set by two matching rules is joined with a comma (see the security-headers note
# below). font-versions.test.mjs holds both.
/fonts/thai/*.woff2
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/fonts/mono/*.woff2
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# The page itself must always be revalidated: it carries the claims, and a stale claim is the
# failure this room exists to prevent.
/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
/404.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

# Security headers on EVERY response (LWK-211): the CSP floor, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy.
#
# ONE rule, on `/*`, and the only rule that may carry these headers. Two reasons.
#  - Coverage: the 404 the platform serves for an UNMATCHED path (`assets.not_found_handling`) is a
#    response to a request for THAT path, so a rule keyed only on `/404.html` may not reach it; a `/*`
#    rule is keyed on the requested path and does. Measured 2026-09-25: on the production alias a
#    missing `/x.svg` came back with the 86400 Cache-Control rule for `/*.svg`, i.e. the path rules
#    are applied to the 404-page fallback. A Workers Preview does NOT serve the fallback at all: an
#    unmatched path there gets a 9-byte platform "Not found" outside the asset pipeline, and no rule
#    of this file (path or host) touches it. Its only header, `X-Robots-Tag: noindex`, is Cloudflare's
#    own. So the preview's 404 is checked for nothing, and production's is checked for everything
#    (scripts/post-deploy-check.mjs).
#  - No duplicates: a header set by two matching rules is JOINED with a comma (Cloudflare's own headers
#    page), which would serve two policies, or one policy twice, and no longer the string written here.
#    surface-check rule 13 fails if a second rule carries the CSP.
# Caps (Cloudflare): 100 rules, 2,000 characters per line. surface-check rule 13 checks both.
#
# WHERE THE OTHER TWO ARE SET. Strict-Transport-Security and X-Content-Type-Options are set by the
# Cloudflare ZONE, not by this file (measured 2026-09-25 on kolwen.com: `max-age=15552000;
# includeSubDomains` and `nosniff`; the workers.dev host carries neither, it does not pass the zone).
# They are deliberately NOT set here: the zone value is the one served on the production host, and a
# second copy here would ride beside it as a joined value nobody can compare to a single declared
# string. post-deploy-check asserts both are PRESENT on the production origin, so a zone setting that
# is switched off is caught, not assumed.
#
# CSP. The pages fetch their typefaces from the site itself: web/fonts/fonts.css is the one manifest every page links, and its @font-face rules load
# the WOFF2 files under /fonts/ only (surface-check rule 13 holds that). So font-src is 'self' alone and the only origin named for a fetch is
# Cloudflare's Web Analytics beacon, below.
#  - script-src: NO 'unsafe-inline'. The one inline script in index.html is admitted by its hash; any
#    byte change breaks it, and surface-check rule 13 recomputes it. 'self' stays for same-origin
#    scripts and Cloudflare's own same-origin /cdn-cgi/ paths; web/ ships no .js file (rule 7).
#    The one named origin is Cloudflare Web Analytics' beacon (LWK-223, owner pick): the edge injects it
#    with a VERSIONED path under https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js/, which an exact-path
#    source (no trailing slash) does not match. CSP path matching treats a source path that ends in `/` as
#    a prefix, so `https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js/` admits that path and any version
#    under it and nothing else on the host. (The host alone would admit every script that host serves.)
#    The unversioned `/beacon.min.js` itself is NOT admitted (W3C CSP3, path-part matching: a source path
#    with more segments than the request path does not match), which matters only if the site ever moves to
#    Cloudflare's manual snippet. surface-check rule 13 holds this exact source string.
#    No `connect-src` is added: with automatic injection the beacon posts to this same origin's
#    /cdn-cgi/rum, which `default-src 'self'` already covers (Cloudflare Web Analytics FAQ, "What do I need
#    to add to my Content Security Policy": for automatic injection "ensure your connect-src includes 'self'").
#    The beacon carries no Subresource Integrity, a named exception to the W3C Subresource Integrity practice for third-party scripts: the FAQ
#    ("How can I enforce Subresource Integrity (SRI) with the JS beacon?") says Cloudflare does not support
#    version-pinning the beacon, so no stable hash exists to pin.
#  - style-src: every inline <style> block of the pages (surface-check rule 13 recomputes them and reports how many it hashed) is admitted by hash too, so there
#    is no 'unsafe-inline' anywhere. The font manifest is a same-origin file, so 'self' admits it; the Google Fonts stylesheet,
#    which varied by user agent and could carry no fixed hash, was the one third-party exception, and self-hosting the two IBM Plex families retired it.
#  - The pricing page (/pricing; it began as /plans, which now answers a 301 from web/_redirects) and the four pages scripts/legal-pages.mjs generates (/terms,
#    /privacy and /refund from the repository's TERMS.md, PRIVACY.md and legal/thai-7day-clause.md, and /changelog from CHANGELOG.md) share ONE <style> block, so
#    the five carry one hash. `node scripts/legal-pages.mjs --check` (a CI step) fails if the pricing page's block is not byte for byte the generated pages'. The page script and
#    the rules for a reader without script are synced blocks, the same bytes on all seven pages, so each adds one hash and no page adds its own. None loads a payment script or a new origin; each takes the `/*` rule
#    like `/`, and no rule here sets noindex on any of them. /llms.txt is plain text and takes only the `/*` rule.
#  - The header and footer rules (HEADER_CSS and FOOTER_CSS in scripts/lib/legal-render.mjs) sit inside the shared block on the pages that generate it, and are written between fixed markers into the style blocks of
#    index.html and 404.html, which are their own; `node scripts/legal-pages.mjs --write` updates the style-src and script-src hashes here, and `--check` fails on a stale one, or on a style-src-elem (none today) that lacks one: `--write` never edits that directive, and surface-check rule 13 judges the directive a browser applies to a block.
#  - The ld+json block in index.html is a DATA block, not script: CSP's script-src does not govern it.
#  - NAMED CONSEQUENCE: on kolwen.com the edge appends an inline bot-detection script to the HTML (the
#    Web Analytics beacon above is a separate, allowed script). It has per-request contents, so no hash can
#    admit it, and this policy blocks it (a console report, nothing on the page). Turning that zone feature off removes the report; allowing it would need
#    'unsafe-inline', which this policy refuses.
# CROSS-ORIGIN HEADERS AND FORM-ACTION (LWK-220 item 2, the OWASP Secure Headers Project set; Cloudflare audit TH-F6).
#  - form-action 'self': a CSP directive with NO default-src fallback, so it is stated. The page has no form today;
#    this keeps one added later from posting off-site. It is part of the CSP floor surface-check rule 13 enforces.
#  - Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy same-origin: the page opens no cross-origin popups and is opened by none that it
#    needs to talk to. Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy same-origin: no other origin embeds this site's responses.
#    Both are checked against the served response by post-deploy-check, like the three headers above.
#  - Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy require-corp is NOT set, on purpose. It makes every cross-origin no-cors
#    subresource need a CORP header. When this was decided the page loaded three: the Google Fonts stylesheet, its font files and
#    the Web Analytics beacon; the fonts are now the site's own, and the beacon is the one cross-origin subresource left. Read 2026-10-02 (curl, HEAD, GET only): the stylesheet, a font file and the
#    unversioned beacon URL each answered `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: cross-origin` and
#    `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, so they would load; but no real browser has loaded this page under COEP
#    yet, the beacon's versioned path was not fetched, and the page has no SharedArrayBuffer or other need for
#    cross-origin isolation. A header that could blank the fonts for a benefit nothing here uses is not shipped
#    on header evidence alone. Revisit with a real-browser run.
#  - upgrade-insecure-requests is NOT set. The page loads no http: subresource (every origin above is https),
#    the zone sends HSTS and redirects http to https, so the directive would upgrade nothing; and it also
#    upgrades same-origin requests, which on a plain-http local preview (wrangler dev) is a behaviour change
#    nobody has measured here.
# Permissions-Policy: every standardized feature the page does not use, names from the W3C
# webappsec-permissions-policy feature registry (read 2026-09-25). The page uses none of them.
# ZONES (the domain seams, web-src/seams.json, surface-check rule 20). This file is the headers file of the COMPANY zone: the one Worker that serves every page of web/, and the
# `/*` rule below is that zone's strict hash policy. Another zone is another Worker with its own publish root and its own headers file, so its
# policy is its own rule set; it is never a second CSP rule here, because Cloudflare joins the values of two matching rules and rule 20 fails the file that tries it.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; script-src 'self' https://static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js/ 'sha256-E7qPAYbONhknD/BCPohidP4Ita9lluXiIgAgddNS2sU='; style-src 'self' 'sha256-yzjIvtJjATLj6yYy1gfv7FPI/iiIcDsPlKDZ4vqKwRM=' 'sha256-3/HimkpZbPqfKx8I5jVZJ6Ak/e7hcDG9TBW2jyMaYKE=' 'sha256-wgurlVqTRc/re5SkuFWBs6HcCX7ZGDI4F1dZji2PY9k=' 'sha256-iqJ978isDCA72Jbdx0ObWs9mPpBxRz1QfgFUpvZYEOE='; font-src 'self'; form-action 'self'
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), autoplay=(), bluetooth=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), encrypted-media=(), fullscreen=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), hid=(), idle-detection=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(), picture-in-picture=(), publickey-credentials-get=(), screen-wake-lock=(), serial=(), sync-xhr=(), usb=(), web-share=(), xr-spatial-tracking=()

# noindex on PREVIEW hosts, never on production (LWK-198, owner ruling 2026-09-23).
# A rule keyed on PATH cannot express this -- `_headers` matches by absolute HOST when the
# left-hand side is an absolute URL (Cloudflare's own docs), so the host itself is the scope.
# This is Cloudflare's own documented example for exactly this need (the "search engines"
# section of the same page): a placeholder matches one dot-delimited host label, whatever it
# contains, so `:version.:subdomain.workers.dev` matches ANY two-label host ending in
# `.workers.dev` -- both a Preview URL (`<preview-name>-kolwen.<subdomain>.workers.dev`) and a
# Version URL (`<version-prefix>-kolwen.<subdomain>.workers.dev`) have exactly that shape.
# PROVEN, not assumed: `kolwen.com` does not end in `.workers.dev`, so no placeholder binding
# can make this rule match it -- verified by a local script re-implementing the documented
# matching semantics against 8 cases, including kolwen.com and www.kolwen.com (no match).
# NAMED RESIDUAL: this also matches kolwen.hetcreep.workers.dev, production's OWN bare
# workers.dev alias -- not kolwen.com, but a production-adjacent surface. (LWK-220 item 3 sets
# `workers_dev: false` in wrangler.jsonc, which disables that alias on the next deploy; this
# note stays true for any host of that shape until then.) Left in rather than narrowed further: no documented `_headers`
# syntax can express "ends with -kolwen" within one label (placeholders are whole-label-only,
# `*` is unconditionally greedy to the end of the pattern) -- narrowing here would be a rule
# whose scope cannot be proven, and an unprovable scope is not shipped.
https://:version.:subdomain.workers.dev/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex
